A||||17||BBBAD GT||Bigger injectors.....||||1||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||01-25-2000||09:18 PM||||I'm new to the Fiero scene (picking up an 87 GT on Sat), but in reading some posts on this board, how does the GM computer know larger injectors are being used, and how does it compensate for it?  <P>I do know the Ford computers can not adjust to bigger injectors without going inside the computer or recalibrating the mass air.<P>I'm new to GM thing (being a Fordman since birth), so any input would be appreciated on how the Fiero fuel injection system works.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||01-25-2000||09:28 PM||||I don't know the injector question here. Just glad to know theres another Fordman here other than me. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||Carrolles||01-25-2000||09:42 PM||||Welcome to the forum.<P>I'll give it s shot.  I don't think the computer necessarily cares what size injectors are installed.  It looks at results meaning it looks at the presence of Oxygen in the exhaust as well as various temperatures and varies the pulse duration of the injectors accordingly.  The problem arises when the injectors can't supply enough fuel to support efficient combustion at high airflow rates in the larger displacement engine.  This results in lack of high end performance.<P>Anyway this is my two cents worth.<P>Tell us a little about yourself.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||baggy||01-25-2000||09:52 PM||||Ford trucks are gonna take on the world..<BR>and my beater will be up front!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||01-25-2000||10:01 PM||||I agree with Carroll. The ECM only sees what its various sensors tell it about what the engine is doing. I beleive you have to go pretty radical (before forced induction)to get outside the parameters of the ECM. Today's ECMs are much more finicky but they also have more sophisticated sensors now.||||reg||14
Z||000005||86SE||01-25-2000||10:31 PM||||FORD, FORD, where??? I love love Ford......  The new 2001 F-150 SuperCrew!!!  Mustang!!...............ahhhhhh I'm better now<P><BR>FORD.............||||reg||5
Z||000006||HF6582||01-25-2000||10:54 PM||||ecm from the factory are set with a pulse width for a specific size injector.<BR>that is why on a mass air car you must change the meter or have it calibrated for the size injector that you will be using.<BR>As for a fiero lets say stock injectors are 19# but you put in 24# injectors, the ecm will keep the same pulse width and dump to much fuel in.<BR>if you need more info just email me direct.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000007||BBBAD GT||01-25-2000||11:00 PM||||<BR>I always thought a certain ECU is programed for a specific injector output (rating) based on a specific pressure.  You are asking alot of the sensors to figure out how big/small its injecotrs are.  I wouldn't think the O2 sensor is good enough to figure out 'how' lean/rich the motor is to tell the computer it has smaller/bigger injectors.  I'm basing all of this on what I know about the Ford ECU (EEC-IV), which is a pretty darn good piece, and what you have to do to run bigger injectors.  <P>Me?  I own a blown 5.0 that runs low 11's at 127 mph at 3400lb that I've built myself, and regularly drive on the street.  I've also tuned it myself without the aid of a dyno/air fuel meter, and have a real good working knowelge of the Ford ECU.  I'm just wondering how the GM product does it.<P>BTW, talking about injector pulse width, I have an Interceptor II that piggy backs the ECU, and shows (and datalogs) lots of functions.  When I had 30lb injectors, my injectors were going 100% duty cycle at only 4600 rpms at 10lb of boost.  I changed to 36lb injectors (with recurving the MAF meter) and now see 100% around 5800 rpms.  <P>  <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>My Web Site (all Ford tho)</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||01-25-2000||11:56 PM||||Good thing we don't have MAF sensors!||||reg||6
Z||000009||HF6582||01-26-2000||12:45 AM||||BBBad,<BR>sent you email<P>I wish that they did have mass air, so much more that you can do with out reprogramming.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000010||geyser||01-26-2000||02:24 AM||||Only the best have Chevy power! Including all you Fiero V6's. As for you, Bad, I'll put my naturally aspirated Corvair ZZ4 up against that blown Stang!||||reg||1
Z||000011||mwbackus||01-26-2000||03:32 AM||||One obvious way would be to reprogram the fuel map vs. load (and rpm) tables on the prom. Another method uses potentiometers in the MAP circuit to fool the ECM into thinking that the load is less than it actually is.  This article illustrates the later idea:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.autospeed.com/A_0008/P_1/article.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autospeed.com/A_0008/P_1/article.html</A> <P>  Engine management systems offered by Accel (DFI), or Haltech (E6GM) would also be excellent choices to accomplish this, although they are quite expensive. <P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000012||BBBAD GT||01-26-2000||07:52 AM||||Lets not get into a pissing contest of who's car is faster or which car manufacture is better, I was simply pointing out my past experience with a Ford product (somebody did ask me say a little about myself).  <P>Mass air sensors are a good thing as pointed out above, I know of an 94 Stang turning low 8's with stock Ford electronics (with a custom chip) and a mass air sensor in FL.  Mass air lets the motor know how much more air is being entered the motor as a result of mods being done without touching the ECU.<P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/</A> <P>Geyser: Tell me more about this naturally aspirated Corvair ZZ4, any details like best ET and specs on the motor?  It must be a thumper to run 10's at close to 130 mph (I would of emailed you, but you don't have an address available here).<p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 01-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||01-26-2000||11:57 AM||||The ECM doesn't *know* you changed injectors, it just sees that the injector pulse width needed before is now different and it will start decreasing the block learn multiplier to get the O2 to swing correctly across 451mV and maintain 14.7:1 AFR. If the injectors are big enough, the BLM will hit it's lower limit of around 108 (I think. I'd have to look.) All of this is done in an attempt to keep the integrator (INT) near 128.<P>Power/Accleration enrichment is another story. It will just flat out be too rich.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000014||daved||01-26-2000||10:11 PM||||Nobody has mentioned what happens when in open loop control, say soon after the engine is started, but before operating temp. is reached.  With the larger injectors, wouldnt the mixture be horribly rich?  The ECU still assumes a given amount of fuel injected (in relation to the throttle position/manifold pressure?) for a certain pulse width using the stock injectors.||||reg||1
Z||000015||1FST2M6||01-27-2000||08:55 AM||||The guys at Custom Network Motors  turbocnm@aol.com Have an Eprom bunrer and access codes to the ADS Superchips BBS. They have the nessasary codes to run just about any injector you want. After about 3 hours of mixing and matching codes and maps for my car (86 V6) CNM got my to not only run but STOMP nuts AND pass emissions on 36#+ injectors. All for a little bit more $ than an ADS strip chip from the Fiero Store. And the computer does not have a set width for the injector pulse. That is dictated by the O2 sensor and wether or not its running efficently @ 14.7:1 (ish) But be careful not to cahnge the impediance of the injectors! If you do you'll smoke the entire computer not just the E-prom.  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 01-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||BBBAD GT||01-27-2000||09:24 AM||||<BR>Wow, 36lb injectors for a motor making 160 rwhp (I think I read that in an earlier post) will have alot of injector left over.  Grant it you (and we all) have 6 cylinders, but I've seen several forced induction V8's (forced induction has a higher demand on the fuel system) with 36lb injectors make 520+ rwhp without the aid of an FMU.  Calculate that to 6 cylinders and you have almost 400 rwhp potential in just the injectors (I'm not sure of the rest of the fuel system that you must consider, like pump/lines/rails).  I'm running 36lb injectors in my 5.0 with an adjustable FMU that calculates to over 550 hp given the 127 mph at 3400lb.  <P>But, if it works for you, thats great.  Even better you passed emissions.  Speaking of emissions, I know several guys in CA that have passed emissions with 77lb injectors with a custom burned chip.<P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||01-28-2000||02:50 PM||||Yeah, the cold engine AFR would need to be changed or it will be rich while warming up.<P>Another issue is the problem of controlling larger injectors at small pulse widths. That's why there are corrections made.<P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-28-2000).]||||reg||1
